Subject: custom-shader-animation = cursor: animate only after the cursor or the focus changes

A cursor shader (a trail, a glide) draws something only for a moment after the cursor moves,
yet `custom-shader-animation = true` keeps the focused terminal's animation loop running for as
long as it is focused: a redraw every 8 ms, and on macOS a display link kept running, while the
terminal sits still.

`cursor` runs the loop like `true` (focused terminal only), but only for a second after the
cursor the shaders see (`iCurrentCursor`, position or color) or the focus changes; the rest of
the time a still terminal draws nothing, as with `false`. The decision is `shaderAnimates`, a
pure function with tests; `last_cursor_change` is stamped where `iTimeCursorChange` is set, and
in `setFocus`, before the display link is synced, so a focus change starts the loop too. When
the second is up, the next draw finds nothing to redraw and the display link stops, as it does
for `false`.

Measured in Calm with an idle focused terminal (M5 Pro, 2026-09-30): 5.9% of a core and 940
wakeups a second with `true`; 0.3% and 21 with `cursor`, the same as `false`. Each long cursor
jump still draws the whole glide: about 18 frames over 145 ms, as with `true`.

Calm's own patch (not from thdxg/ghostty); applies on top of 0005 and 0006.
Zig tests: `zig build test -Dtest-filter=shaderAnimates`.

diff --git a/src/config/Config.zig b/src/config/Config.zig
--- a/src/config/Config.zig
+++ b/src/config/Config.zig
@@ -3230,6 +3230,13 @@
 /// loop will still only run when custom shaders are used. Note that this
 /// will use more CPU per terminal surface and can become quite expensive
 /// depending on the shader and your terminal usage.
+///
+/// This can also be set to `cursor`, for shaders that animate only the
+/// cursor moving or the focus changing (a cursor trail, say): the focused
+/// terminal runs the animation loop for a second after the cursor or the
+/// focus changes (`iTimeCursorChange`, `iTimeFocus`), and a terminal at rest
+/// draws nothing, as with `false`. A shader that animates on `iTime` alone
+/// would stop moving a second after the last change.
 ///
 /// This can be changed at runtime and will affect all open terminals.
 @"custom-shader-animation": CustomShaderAnimation = .true,
@@ -5484,6 +5491,7 @@
     false,
     true,
     always,
+    cursor,
 };
 
